{"judgments": [
  {
    "clause_id": "c001",
    "reasoning_cat1": "The provision says insurance is provided by the company designated by an \"X\" in the box below, but no box or mark appears — only the Zurich name is printed, a mechanical gap between the designation mechanism and the text actually given. The identification of the insurer is otherwise plain and singular, so there is little for two parties to read differently.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "It is consistent with the rest of the certificate, which repeatedly refers to \"the Company\" (claims-made notice, issuance provision) without naming any other insurer; nothing in the Master Policy references contradicts Zurich as insurer.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.12,
    "prob_cat2": 0.05
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c002",
    "reasoning_cat1": "The claims-made-and-reported trigger turns on undefined-here terms (\"Claims\", \"Certificate Period\", \"any applicable Extended Reporting Period\", \"reported in writing to the Company\"), and whether a notice or a suit constitutes a claim first made and reported is the classic litigated question in such policies. The sentence is also mechanically run together with a comma splice before \"DEFENSE COSTS\", though that does not obviously change meaning.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "Its trigger depends entirely on Item 5, whose dates are garbled (\"2/45/2007\" to \"2/AS/2808\"), so the reporting window cannot be fixed from the instrument itself, and it also depends on the Master Policy incorporated by Items 7-8 and the issuance provision.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.38,
    "prob_cat2": 0.30
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c003",
    "reasoning_cat1": "A bare name and street address; nothing in it carries two meanings, and the only oddity is OCR of the label \"Item 1\".",
    "reasoning_cat2": "It is referenced by Item 5 (\"address shown in Item 1\") for the time zone of the period, and the two fit together without tension.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.05,
    "prob_cat2": 0.06
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c004",
    "reasoning_cat1": "\"Protective Life Insurance Company and Empire General and the Registered Representatives of ProEquities\" is a compound designation with an incomplete corporate name (\"Empire General\") and no statement of the scope or capacity in which the Registered Representatives are additional insureds — whether coverage follows the person or only sales of those companies' products is genuinely open. Naming who is insured, and for what, is precisely the sort of phrase two sides read differently.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "It sits against Item 6, which distinguishes \"products of Protective Life and ProEquities, Inc.\" from \"all other covered products\", suggesting broader product coverage than the additional-insured list implies, and against the Master Policy incorporated by the issuance provision.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.48,
    "prob_cat2": 0.38
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c005",
    "reasoning_cat1": "A producer name and mailing address with only transcription noise (\"York Read\", \"c/o a\"); no operative term is capable of two readings.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "Nothing elsewhere in the certificate assigns the producer any role, so there is no provision it can contradict.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.06,
    "prob_cat2": 0.04
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c006",
    "reasoning_cat1": "As written the per-claim limit ($2,060,000.09) exceeds the stated aggregate ($2,000,000.00), an internal numerical mismatch on the face of the item, and the sums themselves are mangled. Limits stated per \"Named Certificate Holder\" rather than per policy also leaves the sharing of limits among multiple insureds unstated.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "The claims-made notice says \"Defense Costs\" are provided in addition to the Limits of Liability, so the two must be read together, but that is a harmonising rather than a conflicting relationship.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.34,
    "prob_cat2": 0.20
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c007",
    "reasoning_cat1": "The dates are unreadable and impossible as written (\"2/45/2007\", \"2/AS/2808\"), and the From/To fields are transposed around the 12:01 A.M. language, so the very period on which a claims-made policy depends cannot be determined from the words. That is a mechanical defect that changes what the provision fixes.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "It is the operative date reference for the claims-made-and-reported condition and for the Item 1 address time-zone rule, so its unintelligibility infects the trigger provision rather than merely standing alone.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.45,
    "prob_cat2": 0.30
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c008",
    "reasoning_cat1": "The two-tier deductible turns on \"covered products\" — an undefined and elastic term — and on the distinction between products \"of Protective Life and ProEquities, Inc.\" and \"all other covered products\", so the boundary of the cheaper tier is contestable. The item presupposes a category of covered products it never enumerates.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "It implies coverage for products beyond those of the entities named as Additional Insureds in Item 2, a tension that can only be resolved by the Master Policy referenced in Items 7-8 and the issuance provision.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.42,
    "prob_cat2": 0.38
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c009",
    "reasoning_cat1": "\"Refer to Master Policy\" states no amount and no term of its own; it is a pointer rather than a promise, and while spare it is not ambiguous in meaning.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "It incorporates an external document while the issuance provision declares that \"this policy embodies all agreements\" between the holder and the Company, a mild integration-versus-incorporation tension.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.14,
    "prob_cat2": 0.22
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c010",
    "reasoning_cat1": "Again a pure cross-reference; endorsements effective at inception are left wholly to another document, which is terse but not internally ambiguous.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "Unnamed endorsements incorporated from the Master Policy could modify any item on this certificate, and the merger language in the issuance provision points the other way, so a relationship problem is plausible.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.15,
    "prob_cat2": 0.24
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c011",
    "reasoning_cat1": "The sentence is truncated mid-clause (\"RELATING TO THIS\"), leaving the reach of the integration undelivered, and it simultaneously deems the application and \"any attachments\" to be the holder's representations while calling this document the whole of the agreement. \"Issued in accordance with the Master Policy\" is itself an unspecified quantum of incorporation.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "It conflicts on its face with Items 7 and 8, which expressly send the reader to the Master Policy for premium and endorsements, and with the claims-made notice's reference to an Extended Reporting Period defined nowhere in this certificate.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.44,
    "prob_cat2": 0.46
  }
]}

